# Changelog

## 1.3.2
Fab-ready release: the hand-solder lines are recorded explicitly and the mated
stack height is calculated from datasheet dimensions. **No part, value, footprint, reference
designator, net or copper feature changed** (`base.kicad_pcb` stays
byte-identical, `9fc20400…`); the schematic is untouched too.

* **Scope correction from the owner:** the module sockets are **hand-soldered by
  the builder**, not ordered from or assembled by JLCPCB. Connector gender is an
  assembly note, not an ordering blocker, so the DO-NOT-ORDER hold is lifted.
* **JLCPCB assembly lines are unchanged at 119 SMD placements / 32 BOM rows**:
  the exporter has always rejected every through-hole footprint, so
  `VSUPPLY_1..12`, `GND1..12`, `INPUT1`, `5V14`, `J7..J18`, the four 3.5 mm jacks
  and the two RV09 pots were never on them. `C2894928` appears on **no**
  assembly line.
* The 44 non-SMD references (25 1x05 sockets + the 2x05 `5V14` + 12 rail-select
  headers + 4 jacks + 2 pots) are now a first-class, checked list:
  `production/hand-solder.json` (hand-maintained input, SHA-256 recorded in the
  manifest), `production/hand-solder.csv` (generated one-row-per-reference list:
  `Qty` = 1 per row, `GroupQty` = that group's size, 44 rows total) and guards in
  `tools/regenerate_production.py` that fail the export on a duplicate reference,
  a group/qty mismatch, an incomplete or over-long list, any hand-solder ref on
  the JLCPCB BOM/CPL, or an exporter exclusion set that differs from the list.
* **The legacy male part number is off the generated BOM without touching
  KiCad.** `LCSC`/`MPN` on the socket symbols cannot be re-substituted without
  also rewriting the same fields inside the pinned, byte-identical
  `base.kicad_pcb`, so the documented per-reference override rewrites only the
  generated `production/bom.csv` to `NOT-JLC`, the convention the jacks and pots
  already use. `C2894928` and `C2894966` no longer appear anywhere in the BOM.
* **Base sockets must be female 1x05 2.54 mm**: 28 module PCBs exist, 27 were
  inspected, and **25 of them carry their power/ground connection as a male
  `Conn_01x05_Pin` header on the bottom side** (4mix and imix mount the same
  header on `F.Cu`; `32bit` cannot be loaded standalone). Recommended builder part: **C2897368 / PM254-1-05-Z-8.5** (HCTL,
  8.5 mm insulator, 6 155 in stock, $0.0826 @150+). Shunt: **C5664** (BOOMELE
  2.00 mm open-top, 3.5 mm tall, 126 400 in stock).
* **Stack height is calculated from datasheet dimensions** (`tools/verify_stack_height.py`,
  `verification/stack-height.json`): jumper envelope 6.0 mm nominal / 6.6 mm
  worst case (2.0 mm insulator + 4.0 mm mating pin; the 2.8 mm solder tail is
  below the board), module underside 11.0 mm nominal (8.5 mm socket + 2.5 mm
  module male insulator), so the **clearance is 5.0 mm nominal / 3.8 mm worst
  case** against a 0.5 mm target. **Minimum socket insulator height 6.3 mm**
  (swallow the worst-case mating pin), i.e. a **6.6 mm nominal part** once the
  X.X ±0.30 tolerance is applied; the same script proves that no module
  bottom-side footprint overlaps the jumper envelope on any of the 12 slots and
  persists its coverage counts and exceptions.
* `tools/verify_power.py` gains an explicit **routed-PCB SHA pin**
  (`routed_pcb_sha256` in `verification/fixed-geometry.json`) so the release
  cannot be regenerated from a moved board; its report and
  `production/manifest.json` now carry `release_status: fab-ready` plus an
  explicit `physical_validation_pending` list instead of the old hold text.
* `production/cost-estimate.json` (new, generated by `tools/estimate_cost.py`)
  records a sourced **partial** component subtotal: **$22.08/board at a 50-board
  batch** ($19.94 JLCPCB-assembled + $2.14 hand-solder), dominated by 12 x
  TPS2111APWR at ≈$16.45/board. It prices 154 of the 163 designators and lists
  the nine it cannot price (5V14, four jacks, two pots, FB1/FB2); PCB fabrication
  and SMT assembly are excluded and need a real quote.
* ERC is still the 8-finding baseline (4 `pin_not_connected`, 3
  `power_pin_not_driven`, 1 `lib_symbol_mismatch`); DRC is still 0 errors / 0
  unconnected / 0 schematic-parity with the same 59 retained
  `lib_footprint_mismatch` warnings. The regenerated gerbers/drills differ from
  the committed ones only in their two export-date comment lines each.
* `tools/verify_power.py` now derives `stack_height_verified` from the current
  `verification/stack-height.json` instead of asserting it, and refuses a
  **stale** report: `stack-height.json` carries SHA-256 fingerprints of
  `hand-solder.json`, `fixed-geometry.json`, both schematics, the PCB and all 27
  inspected module PCBs, and `tools/verify_power.py --selftest` proves the guard
  rejects a changed board and a changed module PCB.
* `tools/verify_stack_height.py` now also walks every top-side through-hole
  footprint on all 27 modules (1 332 parts) and checks its solder tail against
  the jumper envelopes with the tail and module-board tolerances applied
  (1.4 mm nominal -> 1.86 mm worst case, 0.45 mm nearest XY gap, 1.94 mm
  worst-case clearance).
* Open items are listed in POWER.md section 9.6: no assembled stack has been
  measured yet (a mating trial is still advised), the module male header part is
  not annotated on the module PCBs, and 4mix/imix place their power headers on
  `F.Cu` where they cannot mate downwards.

## 1.3.1
Structure-only refactor: the twelve identical per-slot power blocks are drawn
once as a hierarchical template sheet (`slot.kicad_sch`) and instantiated twelve
times (`Slot1` … `Slot12`) from `base.kicad_sch`. Nothing electrical changed.

* **No part added, removed, re-valued or re-sourced.** The exported flat netlist
  is unchanged: the same 78 nets with the same nodes, pin functions and pin types
  (proof: `tools/verify_slot_refactor.py` diffing
  `verification/netlist-before.kicadsexpr` against
  `verification/netlist-after.kicadsexpr`; see POWER.md section 8).
* One template instance = one slot: the 1x05 supply socket (`VSUPPLY_n`), the
  1x05 ground socket (`GND_n`), the TPS2111APWR power mux (`U6..U17`), the 750R
  ILIM resistor (`R28, R30 … R50`), the 100k SEL pulldown (`R29, R31 … R51`),
  the 1uF + 0.1uF rail decoupling (`C22 … C45`) and the 1x02 rail-select header
  (`J7..J18`) - exactly what the flat sheet drew per slot.
* Only two nets are slot specific, so the template exposes exactly two sheet
  pins - `VSLOT` and `SEL` - wired on the parent to the existing global labels
  `VSLOT_n` / `SEL_n`. `5V_SYS`, `+3.3V` and `GND` stay global labels / power
  symbols, as before.
* Reference designators are annotated per sheet instance, so every part keeps the
  reference it had in v1.3.0: Slot1 = U6 / J7 / R28 / R29 / C22 / C23 /
  VSUPPLY_1 / GND1, … Slot12 = U17 / J18 / R50 / R51 / C44 / C45 / VSUPPLY_12 /
  GND12.
* ERC is unchanged (same 8 findings: 4 `pin_not_connected`, 3
  `power_pin_not_driven`, 1 `lib_symbol_mismatch`). `production/bom.csv` and the
  JLCPCB BOM/CPL regenerate identically (163 references / 119 SMD components,
  32 BOM rows).
* **`base.kicad_pcb` is intentionally untouched (byte-identical)**; KiCad still
  reports 0 unconnected items and 0 schematic-parity findings against the new
  schematic.
* One file now serves twelve instances, so the free-text `Description` fields
  that named their own slot ("Slot 4 rail decoupling") became instance-neutral.
  No field that reaches the BOM, CPL, netlist or the board changed.
* No LED is added and no electrical decision is reopened.

## 1.3.0
Power upgrade: input protection, a protected 5 V rail and per-slot rail selection.

* **Input protection** at the USB-C connector: **F1** resettable polyfuse
  (BSMD1812-200-16V, 2 A hold / 4 A trip / 16 V) in series with VBUS and TVS **D2**
  (SMAJ5.0A) on the protected node, both upstream of FB1.
* **New protected 5V_SYS rail**, tapped from protected VBUS (after F1, before FB1) with its
  own polyfuse **F2** (MF-MSMF110/16-2, 1.1 A hold / 2.2 A trip / 16 V), TVS **D3**,
  100 uF/16 V + 0.1 uF bulk and an ERC PWR_FLAG. Protection hierarchy: F1 (2 A hold) protects
  the source/wiring, F2 (1.1 A hold) the 5 V branch, U5's own internal limit the 3.3 V branch.
* **Accepted limitation (documented in POWER.md):** the TVS diodes clamp at 9.2 V at peak
  pulse, above the 6 V absolute maximum of the mux inputs and of U5; VBUS_PROT can therefore
  transiently exceed 6 V during a surge. No rated OVP is fitted in v1.3.0; the planned (not
  implemented) mitigation is a rated OVP switch upstream of both branches.
* **12 x per-slot 2:1 power mux** (**U6..U17**, TPS2111APWR, extended part, 2 482 in stock):
  IN1 = 5V_SYS, IN2 = +3.3 V, OUT = the slot rail feeding all five pins of the existing
  `VSUPPLY_n` socket (net `VSLOT_n`). Manual mode (D0 strapped low), reverse and
  cross-conduction blocking. (The input labels are swapped relative to the original brief so
  that the mandated jumper polarity - shunt = 5 V - is preserved; see POWER.md.)
* **Per-slot current limit** `R28, R30, ... R50 = 750 ohm` (ILIM) -> about 667 mA nominal,
  inside the datasheet-guaranteed 0.63-1.25 A adjustment range of the TPS2111A. The
  odd-numbered `R29, R31, ... R51` are the 100 k SEL pulldowns, not part of the limit.
* **Per-slot selection**: `SEL_n` with a 100 k pulldown and a low-profile 1x02 header
  (**J7..J18**, 2.00 mm, hidden under a mated module). **Shunt fitted = 5V_SYS; shunt absent
  or lost = +3.3 V (fail-safe).** Slot current flows through the mux only - the header carries
  the select level, never slot power.
* Per-mux decoupling 1 uF + 0.1 uF on the slot rail.
* **Sourcing**: LCSC/MPN/Manufacturer fields added to every symbol; `production/bom.csv`
  regenerated and every `TO-VERIFY` line closed. FB1/FB2: the designed GZ2012E800TF is not in
  the JLCPCB library, so the fitted part is the 80 ohm-class equivalent **PZ2012D800-3R0TF**
  (C12389: 80 ohm @100 MHz +/-25 %, 3 A, 40 mohm - same impedance class and same 0805 land as
  the design intent, higher current and lower DCR; same-family 1 A alternative C316425
  GZ2012D800TF). Remaining `NOT-JLC` (4 lines, by design, hand-solder): the four 3.5 mm jacks
  and the two RV09 pots; plans recorded in POWER.md. The per-slot 2.00 mm jumper shunts are a
  user-fit accessory, not on the assembly BOM; the matching stocked part is C5664.
* BOM values fixed (the 24 slot sockets were mislabelled `Conn_02x05_Socket` although they are
  1x05; the SPICE sources V1..V3 are no longer offered for assembly).
* No LED is added for 5 V indication (module firmware indicates its own rail).
* U5, the 3.3 V regulator and the **FB1/FB2 circuit** are functionally unchanged (the beads are
  simply ordered as the stocked 80 ohm equivalent noted above). PCB placement/routing is NOT
  part of this revision - see POWER.md for the Phase 2 constraints.

## 1.2.0
Introduce a new LDO (AP2112K-3.3) which has an EN functionality.
So, we can implement the reset button without using additional components.

Due the older design with a transitor, it won't be able to load mutiple MCUs.

## 1.1.0
Change the push button to a better one.

## 1.0.0
Release 1.0.0

## 0.8.0
Update a footprint of a resistor, so we use the JLCPCB default parts.

## 0.7.0
Add input grounding & remove line_out input buffering

## 0.6.0
Introduce headphone and line out into the base

## 0.5.0

Introduce a RESET button.
Add large capacitors to handle more power fluctuations.

## 0.4.0
Removed power extension via TRS cables. Even with the regulator it will be noisy.
Now we only support GND syncing via TRS.

## 0.3.1

Update the USB-C socket's copper layer masks for proper soldering.

## 0.3.0
Add USB power support to the base.
When connecting multiple base boards, we are sending 5V & the regulator takes care of stepping it down to 3.3v. This will remove some of the critical noise in the setup.


## 0.2.1
Add two Jack sockets each POWER & GND. So, we can cascase if we needed.

## 0.2.0
Add 3.5mm Jack socket for Power & GND delivery between boards.

* Power: Share both 5V & GND between two boards.
* GND: Share only GND between boards

## 0.1.0
Initial Release
